OK, don't forget that the previous MR were fixing 3 things:
- the one you want to remove: very poor performance, not only with PARANOID mode, due to high scanning in buffers (more than 80% improvements due to huge limitation of calling `getByte(pos)` - decreasing from 95% to 15% - )
- small difference of behaviors between Disk and Memory Factories where the undecodedChunk is not released the same way and implies different ways to clean up things, which makes it complicated in Mixed mode obviously (simple guard on `data.refCnt() > 0` is enough but you don't want it)
- another one, tricky but real: when more than 2 HttpData come into the decoder, if the total amount of memory allocated is too high (there was a limit), the buffer is rewritten to remove all "unused" parts, but in fact, in Memory Factory mode, they are all used, so this gives a break on the original data (buffer being rewritten but shared with old HttpData already decoded)

Was: (old lines 343-345)

        if (undecodedChunk != null && undecodedChunk.writerIndex() > discardThreshold) {
            undecodedChunk.discardReadBytes();
        }

Might be: (to mimic the small fix)

        if (undecodedChunk != null && undecodedChunk.refCnt() == 1 && undecodedChunk.writerIndex() > discardThreshold) {
            undecodedChunk.discardReadBytes();
        }



To be complete, in this MR, I found out still an issue: when the separator is in the middle, the algorithm is wrong. I was on going to fix this but I stop right now, due to your decision.

review_comment:
@normanmaurer I choose, for now until other notice, that my new proposal could be a good tradeof:
- keeping current behaviours, even if not really the same on both sides, in order to keep the current existing client codes functional
- adding the relevant guards when releasing buffers cince they are not equivalent everywhere but will tend to allow the same kind of usage, whatever Memory, Mixed or Disk based, and believing that thos guards prevent errors that are not "client" faults, but underlying implementations that are not aligned to each other (mainly Disk and Memory based)
